WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.342 --> 00:00:04.259
- Because I didn't want
to be like my ancestors

00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:07.319
and everyone else.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:10.551
But I felt like my only two choices was to

00:00:10.551 --> 00:00:12.384
either make it or not.

00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:14.737
I didn't want to be a celebrity,

00:00:14.737 --> 00:00:16.821
I didn't want to be an entertainer,

00:00:16.821 --> 00:00:18.542
I wanted to be an artist.

00:00:18.542 --> 00:00:22.325
If it's going to be too
degrading and it's going to be

00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:25.594
something that I can't
live with, I can't do it.

00:00:25.594 --> 00:00:28.676
- She's an American producer
and actress on stage,

00:00:28.676 --> 00:00:30.314
screen and television.

00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:32.391
Her breakthrough film came in 2008

00:00:32.391 --> 00:00:35.385
with her role as a
supporting actress in Doubt

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:38.858
in 2012 is listed by
Time as one of the 100

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:41.108
Most Influential People in the World.

00:00:41.108 --> 00:00:43.026
She's Viola Davis and here's my take

00:00:43.026 --> 00:00:44.947
on her top 10 rules of success.

00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:46.480
Rule number three is my personal favorite

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.358
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:48.358 --> 00:00:50.114
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:50.114 --> 00:00:52.071
Also, as Viola's talking,
if she says something

00:00:52.071 --> 00:00:53.556
that's really meaningful to you,

00:00:53.556 --> 00:00:55.609
really resonates with you, please leave it

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:57.693
in the comments below
and put quotes around it

00:00:57.693 --> 00:01:00.522
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:00.522 --> 00:01:01.355
Enjoy.

00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:07.552
(inspirational music)

00:01:14.468 --> 00:01:18.635
- Because I was brought up in
such a challenging situation

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:22.579
I think that it required me to live

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:27.768
a more examined life if I were to get out.

00:01:30.148 --> 00:01:31.731
I had to dream big.

00:01:33.313 --> 00:01:35.813
There was no room for failure.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.317
So therefore I couldn't

00:01:41.458 --> 00:01:43.125
go the normal route.

00:01:45.091 --> 00:01:49.258
So I found myself at a very
young age living that sort

00:01:50.093 --> 00:01:53.510
of life because I didn't want to be like,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.560
like my ancestors and
everyone else who was like me.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.198
They were, you know, everyone
was either alcoholics

00:02:02.198 --> 00:02:06.565
or they lived in poverty,
they accepted their lot

00:02:06.565 --> 00:02:09.389
in life and I just felt like I
didn't want to live that way.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:13.153
And anybody who wants to
kind of stray from the pack,

00:02:13.153 --> 00:02:16.460
I think has to lead a more examined life.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.617
I did what I had to do in
order to make the dream work.

00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:21.723
I made the commitment
knowing that knowing that it

00:02:21.723 --> 00:02:25.158
always wasn't going to be a smooth ride.

00:02:25.158 --> 00:02:29.284
But I felt like my only
two choices was to either

00:02:29.284 --> 00:02:30.534
make it or not.

00:02:31.778 --> 00:02:34.800
And the not wasn't an option.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.562
So I paid whatever money I need to pay

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:41.981
for the public transportation
to get to acting school

00:02:41.981 --> 00:02:44.454
when I was 15 and I did
whatever I had to do

00:02:44.454 --> 00:02:47.954
to get my undergraduate degree in theater.

00:02:49.285 --> 00:02:52.311
I did the work and I
knew that there was no

00:02:52.311 --> 00:02:53.824
skimping on the work.

00:02:53.824 --> 00:02:57.991
Well, in third grade, this
kid was always teasing me,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.827
I won't mention his name.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:04.302
But he was always teasing
me, calling me you nigga,

00:03:04.302 --> 00:03:06.879
you black nigger, just every single day.

00:03:06.879 --> 00:03:09.824
This is way, way back when so.

00:03:09.824 --> 00:03:12.847
So one day I said I'm going to get him.

00:03:12.847 --> 00:03:17.461
He was considered the fastest
runner in Central Falls.

00:03:17.461 --> 00:03:20.378
And I said I'm going to beat your butt

00:03:21.797 --> 00:03:25.313
I'm going to race you at recess,
you're not the fastest runner

00:03:25.313 --> 00:03:28.220
in Central Falls, I'm the
fastest runner in Central Falls.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.019
I probably flip them the finger,

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:34.112
you know I was a vulgar
kid too, I could give it

00:03:34.112 --> 00:03:36.407
as much as I got it.

00:03:36.407 --> 00:03:40.574
So at recess we decided to
race and the whole school

00:03:42.212 --> 00:03:45.605
was involved, everybody in
the school heard about it

00:03:45.605 --> 00:03:48.553
and all the boys and some of the girls

00:03:48.553 --> 00:03:53.018
were on his side just
kind of screaming for him,

00:03:53.018 --> 00:03:56.851
and I had my three or
four friends on my side.

00:03:57.705 --> 00:04:01.269
And we lived in such abject
poverty I remember that

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:04.794
we wouldn't keep
up-to-date with the shoes,

00:04:04.794 --> 00:04:08.392
so my shoes were maybe a size too small,

00:04:08.392 --> 00:04:11.584
and it was the dead of
winter and I could never,

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.835
I couldn't walk in the
shoes, I'd have to stop

00:04:13.835 --> 00:04:18.247
every two steps, take the
shoes off then put the shoes

00:04:18.247 --> 00:04:22.172
kind of back on and walk again,
they'd hurt my feet so bad.

00:04:22.172 --> 00:04:25.072
And then my socks were sold
torn, only the top part

00:04:25.072 --> 00:04:27.364
of my socks were left.

00:04:27.364 --> 00:04:31.079
So I said how am I going
to race him at recess

00:04:31.079 --> 00:04:32.546
with these shoes?

00:04:32.546 --> 00:04:36.180
So I said I'm going to take my shoes off.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.200
Take my socks off, I'm
going to take my shoes off

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.051
in the dead of winter and the concrete.

00:04:42.051 --> 00:04:43.384
And I raced him.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.687
And I beat him.

00:04:45.687 --> 00:04:48.018
And I thought to myself,
it's only been recently

00:04:48.018 --> 00:04:51.174
that thought to myself
that everybody was cheering

00:04:51.174 --> 00:04:53.546
and cheering and he was
saying, you didn't beat me,

00:04:53.546 --> 00:04:55.138
it was a tie, you didn't beat me!

00:04:55.138 --> 00:04:57.674
I said yes I did, yes I did.

00:04:57.674 --> 00:04:59.148
And then I got in
trouble with the teachers

00:04:59.148 --> 00:05:01.148
for having my shoes off.

00:05:02.757 --> 00:05:06.641
But nobody noticed that I
didn't have any shoes on.

00:05:06.641 --> 00:05:08.935
And that's, it's a metaphor for life

00:05:08.935 --> 00:05:12.823
because nobody knows that
when you come to the race,

00:05:12.823 --> 00:05:16.990
you don't come with the same
set of tools as everyone else.

00:05:18.223 --> 00:05:20.718
That even when you come into the race

00:05:20.718 --> 00:05:24.705
with a deficit you still
have to run the same race.

00:05:24.705 --> 00:05:28.587
And that's a metaphor I use
for my career, you know?

00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:33.344
That you you can't be
perceived even as the black

00:05:33.344 --> 00:05:35.014
actress who doesn't get
the same kind of roles

00:05:35.014 --> 00:05:37.507
as the white actors, you
got to run the same race.

00:05:37.507 --> 00:05:40.286
You got to give the same
quality performances,

00:05:40.286 --> 00:05:44.170
you've got to have the same
standard of excellence,

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.746
even though people know
that you come into the race

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:50.474
in a deficit, that's just
what life is about, you know?

00:05:50.474 --> 00:05:52.605
You know, that's all you
have at the end of the day

00:05:52.605 --> 00:05:56.355
is your work and your
integrity at all costs.

00:05:57.764 --> 00:05:59.935
I don't care if it's
four scenes in a movie

00:05:59.935 --> 00:06:04.102
and I don't care what it is
that you have to infuses it with

00:06:04.962 --> 00:06:06.922
as much excellence as you can.

00:06:06.922 --> 00:06:10.589
So then, absolutely,
when I was able to work

00:06:11.542 --> 00:06:13.592
with Meryl Streep it was a dream come true

00:06:13.592 --> 00:06:16.743
and I knew that I to step my game.

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:19.599
You know, you always get better
when you work with people

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:21.434
who are excellent.

00:06:21.434 --> 00:06:23.446
You almost never get better
when you work with people

00:06:23.446 --> 00:06:25.196
who are not excellent.

00:06:25.196 --> 00:06:27.202
- So when did that dream hit you?

00:06:27.202 --> 00:06:29.982
If it's hitting genesis at
five when did it hit you

00:06:29.982 --> 00:06:31.622
that you wanted to do this?

00:06:31.622 --> 00:06:34.041
- Well, Cicely Tyson, The Autobiography

00:06:34.041 --> 00:06:38.615
of Miss Jane Pittman,
when I seven years old.

00:06:38.615 --> 00:06:40.090
That was it.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.495
That was literally, I
can absolutely mark it.

00:06:43.495 --> 00:06:47.662
Definitively in 128 Washington
Street, in a rat infested,

00:06:49.183 --> 00:06:51.850
not mice infested, rat infested,

00:06:53.243 --> 00:06:57.046
condemned apartment building,
with almost no plumbing,

00:06:57.046 --> 00:06:58.885
every once in awhile,
the plumbing would work.

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:03.265
No phone, lack of food, most
of the time, no electricity.

00:07:03.265 --> 00:07:04.348
No hot water.

00:07:06.006 --> 00:07:09.506
But that one, it was summertime I believe.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.583
And we had some electricity
because we ran the wires

00:07:13.583 --> 00:07:17.015
through the other apartment,
we had that one TV that you put

00:07:17.015 --> 00:07:20.534
the aluminum foil on the antenna.

00:07:20.534 --> 00:07:22.951
And I saw magic, I saw craft.

00:07:25.364 --> 00:07:26.837
And that's what I wanted to do,

00:07:26.837 --> 00:07:28.920
I wanted to be an artist.

00:07:29.765 --> 00:07:31.972
I didn't want to be a
celebrity, I didn't want

00:07:31.972 --> 00:07:33.487
to be an entertainer.

00:07:33.487 --> 00:07:37.327
I wanted to be an artist
because that's what I saw.

00:07:37.327 --> 00:07:41.713
There's always, you always
have to have a boundary

00:07:41.713 --> 00:07:42.778
as an actor.

00:07:42.778 --> 00:07:45.833
For me you, are not going to see me naked.

00:07:45.833 --> 00:07:48.450
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.553
I'm not going to get naked.

00:07:50.553 --> 00:07:54.416
I've turned down a lot of
money to get naked for this

00:07:54.416 --> 00:07:58.416
one television show that
actually never made it,

00:07:59.332 --> 00:08:02.379
because I said I'm not
going to be able to live

00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:04.468
with this if this is kind of preserved

00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:06.218
in celluloid forever.

00:08:07.081 --> 00:08:10.436
But you always have to have
a boundary of what you will

00:08:10.436 --> 00:08:12.314
or will not do.

00:08:12.314 --> 00:08:16.769
Now your boundary is
different from my boundary

00:08:16.769 --> 00:08:19.389
because it's like you start,
you weigh your choices

00:08:19.389 --> 00:08:23.601
and for me if it's going
to be too degrading

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:25.771
and it's going to be something
that I can't live with,

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.492
I can't do it.

00:08:27.492 --> 00:08:28.716
I can't do it.

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:30.581
And even, I can't even do a lot of roles

00:08:30.581 --> 00:08:32.664
where I cuss a lot a lot.

00:08:32.664 --> 00:08:34.512
I'd have do an audition
where I had to really,

00:08:34.512 --> 00:08:38.444
really, I mean, but it was
extraordinary amount of cussing,

00:08:38.444 --> 00:08:39.674
I just couldn't do it.

00:08:39.674 --> 00:08:42.617
And I don't feel like I'm
that puritanical but it's just

00:08:42.617 --> 00:08:44.376
too much for me.

00:08:44.376 --> 00:08:46.876
So if you don't have to
have any boundaries then

00:08:46.876 --> 00:08:50.110
you're going to to be a puppet, you know.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.384
And at the same time, you
have to live with yourself

00:08:53.384 --> 00:08:55.186
at the end of the day.

00:08:55.186 --> 00:08:58.414
You know that's something the actors,

00:08:58.414 --> 00:09:02.392
we do a lot because we want it
so bad so we're willing to do

00:09:02.392 --> 00:09:04.763
anything to get it.

00:09:04.763 --> 00:09:07.786
It's good to have boundaries,
I think it's really good.

00:09:07.786 --> 00:09:11.469
I know that acting is not
rocket science but it is

00:09:11.469 --> 00:09:13.104
an art form.

00:09:13.104 --> 00:09:16.581
And what I find that as the
time has gone on that people

00:09:16.581 --> 00:09:20.179
have kind of forgotten
that it's an art form.

00:09:20.179 --> 00:09:23.206
I had a guy who drove my
husband and I somewhere

00:09:23.206 --> 00:09:27.011
and he said that his
brother was an acting coach,

00:09:27.011 --> 00:09:29.135
and more and more young
people are coming to him

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:31.796
and saying just make me
good enough to get the role.

00:09:31.796 --> 00:09:32.629
- Yeah.

00:09:32.629 --> 00:09:34.604
- And I didn't go win it
for that and I'm not trying

00:09:34.604 --> 00:09:38.291
to make myself look good either
but when I saw Cicely Tyson

00:09:38.291 --> 00:09:42.291
do Jane Pittman, I wanted
to be a crafts person.

00:09:43.733 --> 00:09:46.558
I really understood that it was a craft,

00:09:46.558 --> 00:09:49.260
that I wanted to transform in every role.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:54.162
I wanted to be brave and now
it's gotten harder to do that,

00:09:54.162 --> 00:09:58.217
when, especially as your profile raises,

00:09:58.217 --> 00:09:59.770
it's hard to do that.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:03.177
I mean, I don't mean to sound confusing.

00:10:03.177 --> 00:10:05.470
When you talk to most
actors they will tell you,

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:07.886
oh, this is what I've always wanted to do,

00:10:07.886 --> 00:10:11.203
I love acting, and I do love acting.

00:10:11.203 --> 00:10:13.539
But it's like any commitment.

00:10:13.539 --> 00:10:18.371
You have periods where you
fall out of love with it.

00:10:18.371 --> 00:10:21.560
And along your journey as an actor,

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.727
there are so many moments
you fall out of love with it,

00:10:26.653 --> 00:10:27.486
that you,

00:10:29.588 --> 00:10:33.421
you wonder, you forget
why you wanted to do it

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:39.077
and you wonder if this is
what you're meant to do.

00:10:43.361 --> 00:10:45.855
But basically, it is a love, I guess.

00:10:45.855 --> 00:10:48.884
I think along the way, I've
question the commitment.

00:10:48.884 --> 00:10:50.647
- [Interviewer] So those
moments of questioning come

00:10:50.647 --> 00:10:52.409
when you're doing work
that's not as interesting

00:10:52.409 --> 00:10:54.456
or do they come when
you're doing particularly

00:10:54.456 --> 00:10:55.805
interesting stuff?

00:10:55.805 --> 00:10:59.610
- They come when I'm not doing
things that are interesting.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:04.272
They come when I'm doing
something interesting,

00:11:04.272 --> 00:11:07.955
I think, and it's not received well.

00:11:07.955 --> 00:11:12.122
They come when the business
strangles my love of it.

00:11:16.629 --> 00:11:19.453
Those are the moments
that come and you forget

00:11:19.453 --> 00:11:21.091
why you love it.

00:11:21.091 --> 00:11:22.356
You do.

00:11:22.356 --> 00:11:26.523
And sometimes the memory
lapse can last a long time.

00:11:28.003 --> 00:11:30.165
And then you think OK, there's
got to be something else

00:11:30.165 --> 00:11:32.574
that's going to come in it this just,

00:11:32.574 --> 00:11:36.741
comes in my life that's going
to wake me up in the morning.

00:11:37.655 --> 00:11:39.693
That's the journey of an actor though,

00:11:39.693 --> 00:11:41.291
I guess that's what it is.

00:11:41.291 --> 00:11:42.638
- [Interviewer] Well, I guess
that anything that you love

00:11:42.638 --> 00:11:45.498
that becomes a job, it hurts.

00:11:45.498 --> 00:11:48.622
It doesn't have that same...

00:11:48.622 --> 00:11:50.644
I mean, obviously a lot of
people just go through life

00:11:50.644 --> 00:11:52.648
working for the weekend, as the song says.

00:11:52.648 --> 00:11:53.795
- Yeah, exactly.

00:11:53.795 --> 00:11:54.745
- [Interviewer] But if you
have things that you're

00:11:54.745 --> 00:11:56.462
passionate about, that you're working for,

00:11:56.462 --> 00:11:59.591
reexamining it and finding it again,

00:11:59.591 --> 00:12:00.453
- Yes.

00:12:00.453 --> 00:12:01.434
- [Interviewer] It's certainly
a challenge we all go through

00:12:01.434 --> 00:12:03.107
in whatever profession
over and over again.

00:12:03.107 --> 00:12:04.746
- Yeah and I guess that's what it is,

00:12:04.746 --> 00:12:07.579
I went through that long diatribe.

00:12:08.454 --> 00:12:09.733
- [Interviewer] No, it was great diatribe.

00:12:09.733 --> 00:12:11.573
- But really, that's what it is.

00:12:11.573 --> 00:12:13.656
You question the passion.

00:12:15.298 --> 00:12:17.963
You know it's like any commitment,

00:12:17.963 --> 00:12:19.968
you got to work on the passion, too.

00:12:19.968 --> 00:12:22.994
You got to work to bring the passion back.

00:12:22.994 --> 00:12:25.744
- With Doubt, which might be
the first time that people

00:12:25.744 --> 00:12:28.816
in film necessarily really
appreciated what you're doing.

00:12:28.816 --> 00:12:30.613
(audience applauding)

00:12:30.613 --> 00:12:34.780
Your preparation for that
was astronomical, amazing

00:12:36.707 --> 00:12:39.293
and so the last the
last pre-Help question,

00:12:39.293 --> 00:12:42.013
if I can just ask you just
to show the kind of work

00:12:42.013 --> 00:12:43.563
that you're willing to do for even,

00:12:43.563 --> 00:12:46.064
I think it was eight
minutes of screen time,

00:12:46.064 --> 00:12:48.350
which was deservedly rewarded
with an Oscar nomination,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:51.218
but I mean that is not a ton
to work with and yet you made

00:12:51.218 --> 00:12:52.244
the most of it.

00:12:52.244 --> 00:12:55.577
What did you do to prepare for that one?

00:12:56.706 --> 00:12:59.402
- Well, it's based on a
play so I read the play.

00:12:59.402 --> 00:13:02.397
After the play I said I don't
even understand this woman.

00:13:02.397 --> 00:13:04.729
I don't know why this
got all this attention,

00:13:04.729 --> 00:13:07.760
this woman wants to sacrifice
her son and da, da, da, da.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.445
And I had all the kind of
responses that you would have

00:13:11.445 --> 00:13:14.599
with character that you
didn't understand but it's,

00:13:14.599 --> 00:13:18.682
being an actor you have
to do investigative work.

00:13:19.567 --> 00:13:23.012
You have to, you have to make sure that

00:13:23.012 --> 00:13:27.179
when people are in the
audience they understand you.

00:13:28.047 --> 00:13:30.964
And they ultimately feel something.

00:13:31.857 --> 00:13:36.433
And so I had to write a bio
which ended up being over

00:13:36.433 --> 00:13:38.149
a hundred pages.

00:13:38.149 --> 00:13:41.506
I talk to people who had seen
the play because I was doing

00:13:41.506 --> 00:13:44.209
a play at the same time
and never saw the play.

00:13:44.209 --> 00:13:46.253
I read the script probably I don't know

00:13:46.253 --> 00:13:47.728
how many times.

00:13:47.728 --> 00:13:52.272
So literally study the
script for four months before

00:13:52.272 --> 00:13:56.453
I finally got it, like
I had the aha moment.

00:13:56.453 --> 00:14:00.924
And the aha moment is we
always want to feel that

00:14:00.924 --> 00:14:02.674
decisions are pretty.

00:14:04.197 --> 00:14:07.835
We always want to believe
that decisions are easy and

00:14:07.835 --> 00:14:10.835
sometimes they're a Sophie's Choice.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:12.740
You know?

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:16.907
And sometimes a person's
motivations are absolutely pure,

00:14:18.469 --> 00:14:22.636
but to the outside eye, they
look very dysfunctional.

00:14:23.621 --> 00:14:27.178
And I probably, you
know, had the aha moment

00:14:27.178 --> 00:14:29.921
two days before even started
filming and I was really

00:14:29.921 --> 00:14:32.947
waiting for that aha moment
because I said I'm going to be

00:14:32.947 --> 00:14:36.118
opposite Meryl Streep and
she's going to tear me

00:14:36.118 --> 00:14:37.201
a new A-hole.

00:14:38.407 --> 00:14:43.325
And I do not want to look bad
so, but I had that aha moment.

00:14:43.325 --> 00:14:45.658
But I did my work with that.

00:14:47.005 --> 00:14:50.607
I can't emphasize that enough, you know?

00:14:50.607 --> 00:14:54.263
Because I never want anyone
to dismiss my talent.

00:14:54.263 --> 00:14:57.775
Listen I may get up in the
screen and there are times

00:14:57.775 --> 00:15:01.210
where may stink because
it's occupational hazard

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:05.662
to not always be good, but
I never want anyone to say

00:15:05.662 --> 00:15:07.750
that she's not an actress.

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.942
I'm an artist, I consider
myself to be an artist,

00:15:10.942 --> 00:15:12.525
first and foremost.

00:15:14.493 --> 00:15:17.660
And when I was given Annalise Keating,

00:15:18.569 --> 00:15:21.715
I tell this story a lot
that I saw the pilot

00:15:21.715 --> 00:15:25.449
and I was horrified 'cause
you got to look at yourself,

00:15:25.449 --> 00:15:28.949
with my lashes and my little chubby waste,

00:15:31.016 --> 00:15:33.514
that I thought was a very chubby waste.

00:15:33.514 --> 00:15:37.770
And I didn't look the way
I thought I would look.

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:40.596
I had an idea in my head when
I thought of the adjectives,

00:15:40.596 --> 00:15:42.763
sexy, dynamic, mysterious!

00:15:44.764 --> 00:15:48.576
You know, and I didn't see
sexy, dynamic, mysterious.

00:15:48.576 --> 00:15:50.901
- [Co-Panelist] We did, they're right.

00:15:50.901 --> 00:15:53.651
- But no, what I'm leading to is,

00:15:56.312 --> 00:15:57.562
I said why not?

00:15:59.136 --> 00:16:02.641
And I think that that's
where it all beings.

00:16:02.641 --> 00:16:05.740
Is that question of why not?

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:07.663
Why can't I be sexy?

00:16:07.663 --> 00:16:10.069
I reject the notion, in the past,

00:16:10.069 --> 00:16:12.617
that dark skin women cannot be beautiful.

00:16:12.617 --> 00:16:16.548
I reject the fact that
if I take my wig off

00:16:16.548 --> 00:16:18.715
and do a whole scene with my fro,

00:16:18.715 --> 00:16:20.882
that somehow that's taboo.

00:16:21.821 --> 00:16:25.131
I reject the fact that
I have to be a size two.

00:16:25.131 --> 00:16:27.627
I reject the fact that
I have to be likable,

00:16:27.627 --> 00:16:29.223
I'd rather be interesting.

00:16:29.223 --> 00:16:32.119
Nobody asked Robert De Niro in Taxi Driver

00:16:32.119 --> 00:16:36.047
or James Gandolfini, or Anthony Hopkins.

00:16:36.047 --> 00:16:38.785
You know, were you concerned
that you were likable

00:16:38.785 --> 00:16:40.236
in Silence of the Lambs?

00:16:40.236 --> 00:16:43.236
(audience laughing)

00:16:44.451 --> 00:16:46.419
(audience applauding)

00:16:46.419 --> 00:16:49.164
That somehow, you have
to reject all the things

00:16:49.164 --> 00:16:52.192
that people have told you you were.

00:16:52.192 --> 00:16:54.525
And I think it starts there.

00:16:55.589 --> 00:16:57.761
- That's amazing, I don't think,

00:16:57.761 --> 00:16:59.931
who can follow that?

00:16:59.931 --> 00:17:00.968
On that note...

00:17:00.968 --> 00:17:04.648
(audience applauding)

00:17:04.648 --> 00:17:06.040
- Thank you guys, so much for watching.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:09.103
I made this video because
Sheryl Chapman asked me to.

00:17:09.103 --> 00:17:10.950
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.150
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:14.171
and I'll see what I can do.

00:17:14.171 --> 00:17:16.459
I'd also love to know,
what was the most impactful

00:17:16.459 --> 00:17:17.727
part of this video for you?

00:17:17.727 --> 00:17:19.808
What lesson resonated the most with you

00:17:19.808 --> 00:17:21.888
and you're going to carry
that forward into your life

00:17:21.888 --> 00:17:22.873
and your business.

00:17:22.873 --> 00:17:24.349
Please leave it in the comments below,

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:26.030
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:28.020
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Davina Lyons.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:30.147
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:17:30.147 --> 00:17:31.084
Your One Word.

00:17:31.084 --> 00:17:32.802
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:17:32.802 --> 00:17:35.676
Thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:17:35.676 --> 00:17:37.925
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:17:37.925 --> 00:17:41.355
And whatever your one word is, much love.

00:17:41.355 --> 00:17:42.855
I'll see you soon.

00:17:48.933 --> 00:17:51.183
- In my mind, I see a line.

00:17:54.219 --> 00:17:58.386
And over that line I see green
fields and lovely flowers

00:18:00.025 --> 00:18:05.025
and beautiful white women
with their arms stretched out

00:18:05.025 --> 00:18:07.604
to me over that line.

00:18:07.604 --> 00:18:10.091
But I can't seem to get there no how.

00:18:10.091 --> 00:18:13.733
I can't seem to get over that line."

00:18:13.733 --> 00:18:16.816
That was Harriet Tubman in the 1800s.

00:18:17.992 --> 00:18:19.876
And let me tell you something.

00:18:19.876 --> 00:18:22.940
The only thing that
separates women of color

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:25.607
from anyone else is opportunity.

00:18:26.821 --> 00:18:30.071
(audience applauding)

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:38.977
You cannot win an Emmy for
roles that are simply not there.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.733
(audience applauding)

00:18:42.733 --> 00:18:43.566
So here's

00:18:45.612 --> 00:18:48.779
to all the writers, the awesome people

00:18:50.272 --> 00:18:54.105
that are Ben Sherwood,
Paul Lee, Peter Nowalk,

00:18:55.372 --> 00:18:56.539
Shonda Rhimes.

00:18:57.809 --> 00:19:01.082
People who have redefined what it means

00:19:01.082 --> 00:19:04.891
to be beautiful, to be
sexy, to be a leading woman,

00:19:04.891 --> 00:19:06.611
to be black.

00:19:06.611 --> 00:19:08.581
(audience applauding)

00:19:08.581 --> 00:19:11.202
And to the Taraji P. Hensons,

00:19:11.202 --> 00:19:14.063
the Kerry Washingtons, the Halle Berrys,

00:19:14.063 --> 00:19:17.230
the Nicole Beharies, the Meagan Goods.

00:19:18.067 --> 00:19:20.478
To Gabrielle Union.

00:19:20.478 --> 00:19:23.728
Thank you for taking us over that line.

00:19:24.615 --> 00:19:27.888
Thank you to the Television Academy.

00:19:27.888 --> 00:19:28.721
Thank you.

00:19:28.721 --> 00:19:31.527
What is there but a dream,
you can't trade in your dream

00:19:31.527 --> 00:19:33.027
for another dream.

00:19:34.188 --> 00:19:37.260
I am so proud to be an actor and I thank

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:40.405
The Screen Actors Guild for this award.

00:19:40.405 --> 00:19:43.101
And I have a special,
special note to the kids

00:19:43.101 --> 00:19:46.601
in Central Falls, Rhode
Island and Segue Institute

00:19:46.601 --> 00:19:49.136
of Learning in Central
Falls, Rhode Island.

00:19:49.136 --> 00:19:51.636
To dream big and dream fierce.

00:19:52.813 --> 00:19:54.213
Thank you very much.

00:19:54.213 --> 00:19:55.626
(audience applauding)

00:19:55.626 --> 00:19:59.188
So my question is, Senator
Truco, did you have

00:19:59.188 --> 00:20:01.188
Trisha Stanley murdered?

00:20:04.056 --> 00:20:05.404
- Are you out of your mind?

00:20:05.404 --> 00:20:07.164
- Your Honor, this has
gone on long enough.

00:20:07.164 --> 00:20:10.025
- Did you convince your tenant
Linda Morelli to testify

00:20:10.025 --> 00:20:12.722
that Trisha Stanley was
killed by her boyfriend,

00:20:12.722 --> 00:20:13.703
David Allen?

00:20:13.703 --> 00:20:15.587
- You are way off base, I
recommend you rein this in.

00:20:15.587 --> 00:20:18.406
- Explain why your eviction
suit against Ms. Morelli

00:20:18.406 --> 00:20:20.653
was dropped despite her
inability to pay rent.

00:20:20.653 --> 00:20:21.486
- [Judge] Ms. Keating.

00:20:21.486 --> 00:20:23.190
- Is it because her lost rent
money was nothing compared to

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.346
the millions you made once Ms.
Stanley was out of your way?

00:20:25.346 --> 00:20:26.452
(gavel banging)

00:20:26.452 --> 00:20:27.285
- Answer the question, Senator.

00:20:27.285 --> 00:20:28.187
- [Judge] Ms. Keating, enough.

00:20:28.187 --> 00:20:30.025
- Why was Linda Morelli
granted these concessions?

00:20:30.025 --> 00:20:32.975
- Mr. Chief Justice, I have
extended great deference

00:20:32.975 --> 00:20:35.515
to the court by showing up here today.

00:20:35.515 --> 00:20:37.514
But I am not going to put
up with this any longer.

00:20:37.514 --> 00:20:40.170
- I am sorry, senator, if you
do not have the time to answer

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:42.138
for who bore the real
cost of your development.

00:20:42.138 --> 00:20:43.121
- [Defense] Objection!

00:20:43.121 --> 00:20:44.472
- Not just Trisha Stanley
and her boyfriend,

00:20:44.472 --> 00:20:47.821
David Allen, who served
21 years in prison.

00:20:47.821 --> 00:20:48.654
- [Defense] Your Honor, please!

00:20:48.654 --> 00:20:49.903
- But the thousands who
were displaced in the name

00:20:49.903 --> 00:20:51.093
of lining your pockets!

00:20:51.093 --> 00:20:52.115
- [Judge] Ms. Keating!

00:20:52.115 --> 00:20:53.835
- The majority of whom are
poor, powerless, and didn't bear

00:20:53.835 --> 00:20:56.124
the color of skin desirable
to your business interests.

00:20:56.124 --> 00:20:57.508
- [Judge] That's enough, Ms. Keating!

00:20:57.508 --> 00:20:58.924
- You tore a community apart.

00:20:58.924 --> 00:20:59.893
- [Judge] Ms. Keating, I want order!

00:20:59.893 --> 00:21:01.526
- You tore families apart,
you destroyed lives, Senator.

00:21:01.526 --> 00:21:02.383
- [Judge] Ms. Keating!

00:21:02.383 --> 00:21:04.199
- Admit it., admit you
killed Trisha Stanley

00:21:04.199 --> 00:21:07.480
and had my client, David
Allen, set up for her murder.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:08.911
- That is enough!

00:21:08.911 --> 00:21:09.973
You hear me?

00:21:09.973 --> 00:21:11.900
You say another word, and
I'll have no choice but to

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:13.400
throw you in jail.

00:21:24.476 --> 00:21:26.729
- We need a dozen more.

00:21:26.729 --> 00:21:30.003
- Me and Aibileen done
asked everybody we know.

00:21:30.003 --> 00:21:30.836
31 maids.

00:21:31.929 --> 00:21:34.837
They all too scared, think we crazy.

00:21:34.837 --> 00:21:36.066
- Well,

00:21:36.066 --> 00:21:38.806
if we don't get more, we're
not getting published.

00:21:38.806 --> 00:21:41.215
- I gots plenty a stories, Miss Skeeter.

00:21:41.215 --> 00:21:45.016
Just write 'em down and
invent the maid that said it.

00:21:45.016 --> 00:21:46.083
You're already making up names.

00:21:46.083 --> 00:21:47.719
Just make up the maids, too.

00:21:47.719 --> 00:21:49.641
- We're not going to do that.

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:51.807
That would be wrong.

00:21:51.807 --> 00:21:53.769
- Don't give up on this, Miss Skeeter.

00:21:53.769 --> 00:21:55.686
- It wouldn't be real.

00:21:56.553 --> 00:21:58.303
- They killed my son.

00:22:05.832 --> 00:22:09.810
He fell carrying two by fours at the mill.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.180
Truck run over him, crushed his lung.

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:13.097
- Aibileen.

00:22:16.931 --> 00:22:18.235
- That white foreman threw his body on

00:22:18.235 --> 00:22:20.660
the back of a truck.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:23.522
Drove to the colored hospital.

00:22:23.522 --> 00:22:26.772
Dumped him there and honked the horn.

00:22:35.978 --> 00:22:38.482
There was nothin' they could do so

00:22:38.482 --> 00:22:40.399
I brought my baby home.

00:22:44.955 --> 00:22:48.205
Laid him down on that sofa right there.

00:22:51.094 --> 00:22:53.511
He died right in front of me.

00:22:55.427 --> 00:22:57.179
Just 24 years old, Miss Skeeter.

00:22:57.179 --> 00:22:59.762
Best part of a person's life.

00:23:00.778 --> 00:23:03.195
Anniversary of his death come

00:23:04.387 --> 00:23:07.007
every year and I can't breathe.

00:23:07.007 --> 00:23:10.840
But to you all it's just
another day of bridge.

00:23:13.633 --> 00:23:14.800
You stop this,

00:23:16.414 --> 00:23:18.096
everything I wrote, he wrote,

00:23:18.096 --> 00:23:21.179
everything he was going to die with him.

